There is no mathematics on this page. It is a reading excerpt in Riemann's hand, continuing the previous folio. The source is J. F. Herbart, Psychologie als Wissenschaft, 2nd analytic part, 2nd section, ch. 3 ('Von unserer Auffassung der Welt und den damit verbundenen Täuschungen'), Werke VI pp. 264-270, §§140-141. Riemann copies or condenses the following passages, mostly verbatim with ellipses.

(1) p. 264. Herbart praises Locke ('Locken') for noticing that the sensible marks (Merkmale) of a thing are found together only as an accidental aggregate. He quotes Locke, Essay, Book IV ch. VI §7: complex ideas of substances are collections of qualities observed to coexist in an unknown substratum. Herbart's criticism is that Locke presents the substratum as something given by observation, whereas it is only added in thought ('hinzugedacht, aber nicht gegeben'). Riemann's struck-through words show him condensing the passage. Herbart then dismisses the scholastic definition (substance = what exists only as subject, never as predicate) as a mere nominal definition. He attacks Kant for making substance the 'permanent' (das Beharrliche) by calling on time in order to apply an a priori category to experience. On Herbart's view, the true concept of substance, which arises from experience, is timeless.

(2) pp. 267-268. A related point from Locke: no sensible mark belongs to the thing outright, because conditions are needed for it to appear (light for colour, vibrating air for sound). Riemann notes the Locke references: IV.6.11; II.23.7; IV.3.7; II.21.3. Then a Leibniz remark: knowledge of things in concreto precedes knowledge of abstracta ('we know the warm before warmth'), and the distinction between the concrete and the abstract.

(3) §140, pp. 269-270. The concepts of substance and force are not fixed innate categories. They are changeable products of experience, still unfinished, and will settle only when metaphysics matures. Hence the human conception of the world is 'im Werden begriffen'. It follows that the illusions arising along the way vary with the stage of development and cannot be confined to a register such as Kant's antinomies. Because abstraction follows different paths (§151), the mind finds as 'the real' in turn: atoms, Platonic ideas, Pythagorean numbers, an absolute One, a creator God, substances as bearers of properties, living monads, permanent bearers of change.

(4) §141. Every original perception (red, sweet, ...) is positing or affirmative. The mutual inhibition (Hemmung) of presentations produces negation and doubt, and leads to separating properties (with merely inherent being) from things. Reality then 'wanders' successively:
- from properties to things;
- from things to elements (fire, water, air, earth);
- from these to chemical elements (Feuerstoff, Wasserstoff, Stickstoff);
- and finally, for the idealist, to the Ich, behind which everything else holds only borrowed reality.

The interest is purely biographical and philosophical: Riemann's close study of Herbart's psychology and metaphysics. This study is known to have influenced his Naturphilosophie and his notion of 'Mannigfaltigkeit'. The previous page, on Herbart's 'Reihenformen' and qualitative continua, is the more relevant one for the Habilitationsvortrag.
